     "text": "STEWART BENJAMIN AND SINGLETON MEDICAL GROUP - DISCHARGE SUMMARY\n\nPatient: Eric Moran\nDOB: 02/21/2007\nSex: M\nMRN: 933687\n\nAdmit Date: 03/08/2024\nDischarge Date: 03/10/2024\n\nPrincipal Diagnosis: Adrenal insufficiency / pituitary adenoma (kind adrenal)\n\nSecondary Diagnoses: None\n\nAttending Physician: Dr. David Johnson, Endocrinology\n\nReason for Admission: The patient, Eric Moran, was admitted on 03/08/2024 due to the presence of adrenal insufficiency secondary to a pituitary adenoma. This condition was identified and managed during his annual physical on 10/16/2024, where symptoms consistent with adrenal insufficiency were noted and confirmed through subsequent testing.\n\nHospital Course: The hospital course for Eric was largely uneventful. Upon admission, his vital signs were stable with a blood pressure of 116/74 mmHg and a weight of 111 lbs. He reported no acute distress and was cooperative with the medical team. Given his known history of adrenal insufficiency, he was started on fludrocortisone 0.1 mg daily to manage his condition. Throughout his stay, his condition remained stable with no significant changes in his vital signs or laboratory results. He was monitored closely for any signs of adrenal crisis, but none were observed. \n\nDuring his stay, routine blood tests were performed to assess his adrenal function and overall health. These tests showed stable renal function, normal electrolyte levels, and no acute abnormalities. His blood pressure remained within normal limits throughout his hospitalization, and he did not report any significant pain or discomfort.\n\nProcedures: No surgical procedures were performed during this admission. The focus was on the medical management of his adrenal insufficiency with fludrocortisone.\n\nDischarge Medications: \n- Fludrocortisone 0.1 mg daily\n\nDischarge Plan: Eric was discharged home in stable condition on 03/10/2024. He was advised to continue his current medication regimen and to follow up with his endocrinologist, Dr. David Johnson, for ongoing management of his adrenal insufficiency and pituitary adenoma. He was instructed to return to the emergency department if he experiences symptoms of adrenal crisis, such as severe nausea, vomiting, weakness, or confusion.\n\nSocial History: Eric reports current use of cigars, smoking approximately 8 cigars per year, a habit he started on 06/03/2009. He consumes about 12 alcoholic drinks per week. His family history is significant for his mother, who has COPD diagnosed at age 69.\n\nFollow-Up: A follow-up appointment has been scheduled with Dr. Johnson in one month to reasses"
